Verify JWTs with RS256 and a cached JWKS endpoint
Sign with a private key held by the auth service; every other service verifies with public keys fetched from JWKS by kid.
HS256 shared secret was copied into six services and could not be rotated without a deploy.
Symmetric signing makes every verifier also a potential issuer.
Switch to RS256, publish /.well-known/jwks.json, cache keys for 10 minutes keyed by kid.
Reject tokens whose alg is not in an explicit allow-list.
## Solution

```ts
const jwks = createRemoteJWKSet(new URL(process.env.JWKS_URL!), {
  cacheMaxAge: 10 * 60 * 1000,
});
const { payload } = await jwtVerify(token, jwks, {
  algorithms: ["RS256"],          // never trust the header's alg
  issuer: "https://auth.acme.test",
  audience: "acme-api",
});
```

Rotation: publish the new key alongside the old one, start signing with the new `kid`, remove the old after the longest token TTL.

Guard async effect results with an ignore flag in cleanup
A local `ignore` boolean set in the cleanup stops late responses from calling setState.
Stale async results overwrote fresh state when props changed quickly.
No way to discard a response once the effect was superseded.
let ignore = false; set it true in the cleanup; check before setState.
Check the flag before every setState after an await.
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  let ignore = false;
  load(id).then(data => { if (!ignore) setData(data); });
  return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [id]);
```

Prefer AbortController when the request itself can be cancelled; the flag only drops the result.

Abort stale fetches in useEffect to avoid out-of-order results
Responses can arrive out of order; cancel the previous request when the dependency changes.
Typing quickly showed results for a stale query.
Slower earlier request resolved after the later one and overwrote state.
Create an AbortController per effect run and abort it in the cleanup.
Every fetch in an effect gets a signal.
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const ac = new AbortController();
  fetch(`/api/search?q=${q}`, { signal: ac.signal })
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(setResults)
    .catch(e => { if (e.name !== "AbortError") throw e; });
  return () => ac.abort();
}, [q]);
```

useEffect runs twice in dev StrictMode; make effects idempotent
StrictMode mounts, unmounts and remounts to flush out missing cleanup; do not suppress it, fix the effect.
Duplicate network calls and subscriptions in development only.
Effect had no cleanup so the simulated unmount left the first subscription alive.
Return a cleanup function that undoes the setup.
If an effect has setup, it needs the matching teardown.
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const ws = new WebSocket(url);
  ws.onmessage = onMessage;
  return () => ws.close();       // StrictMode relies on this
}, [url]);
```

Production runs the effect once; do not "fix" it with a ref guard, that hides real leaks.

Playwright: rely on auto-waiting locators, never waitForTimeout
Web-first assertions retry until the condition holds; fixed sleeps either waste time or flake.
e2e suite had a 7 percent flake rate on shared CI runners.
Tests used page.waitForTimeout(2000) instead of waiting for state.
Replace sleeps with expect(locator).toBeVisible() style assertions.
No waitForTimeout in committed tests.
```ts
// before
await page.click("text=Save");
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);
// after
await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Save" }).click();
await expect(page.getByText("Saved")).toBeVisible();
```

Add `retries: 1` plus `trace: "on-first-retry"` so the remaining flakes leave evidence.
